course: world
lesson: 87
title: Electricity at home and staying safe
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Electricity for our homes is made in power stations, wind farms and solar farms. It travels along thick cables on tall pylons at very high voltage, a very strong electrical push, then a substation lowers the voltage before it reaches our streets and houses.
explain: Inside a house, wires hidden in the walls carry electricity to sockets and lights. A meter counts how much electrical energy the home uses, and the family pays for it. Things that heat, like kettles, ovens and heaters, use far more electricity than a phone charger or an LED bulb.
explain: Every home has safety devices in its fuse box. A fuse or circuit breaker cuts off the electricity if too much flows, for example when a cable is damaged or too many things are plugged in, because too much current makes wires hot enough to start a fire.
explain: Mains electricity can kill. Keep water away from plugs and sockets, never use a cable that is cracked or frayed, never climb pylons or go into substations, and keep kites and fishing rods far from overhead power lines. If a power line has fallen, stay well away and tell an adult at once.
explain: If someone is getting an electric shock, do not touch them, or the electricity can pass into you too. First switch off the power at the main switch, then call for help.
fact: Power stations, wind farms and solar farms make electricity for homes.
fact: Pylons carry electricity long distances at very high voltage.
fact: A substation lowers the voltage before electricity goes to homes.
fact: An electricity meter measures how much electrical energy a home uses.
fact: Things that make heat, like kettles and heaters, use a lot of electricity.
fact: A fuse or circuit breaker cuts off the electricity if too much flows.
fact: Plugging too many things into one socket can overheat the wires and cause a fire.
fact: A cracked or frayed cable can give an electric shock.
fact: Pull out a plug by holding the plug itself, because pulling the cable can damage the wires inside.
fact: Water and electricity are dangerous together, so wet hands must never touch plugs or switches.
fact: You should never fly a kite near overhead power lines.
fact: If someone is getting an electric shock, switch off the power before touching them.
fact: Turning off lights and devices that are not in use saves electricity and money.
exercise: How does electricity travel from power stations across the country? | Along cables on tall pylons | Through water pipes under the ground | Through the air as invisible light | In big batteries carried by lorries
exercise: What does a substation do? | Lowers the voltage before electricity reaches homes | Makes electricity by burning coal | Stores electricity for the winter | Turns electricity into gas for cooking
exercise: What does an electricity meter in a home do? | Measures how much electricity is used | Makes extra electricity at night for the house | Stops electricity from flowing into the house | Protects the whole house from lightning strikes
exercise: Which uses the most electricity? | An electric heater | A small LED bulb left on | A phone charger in the wall | A digital clock on the oven
exercise: What does a fuse or circuit breaker do? | Cuts off the power if too much electricity flows | Makes the electricity stronger when needed | Stores spare electricity for later | Turns the lights on when it gets dark
exercise: Why is it dangerous to plug many things into one socket? | The wires can overheat and start a fire | The things will all stop working at once | The electricity runs out more quickly | The socket will become magnetic
exercise: What should you do with a cable whose plastic is cracked? | Stop using it and tell an adult | Wrap it in wet paper and carry on | Use it only for short times | Tie a knot in it to hold it together
exercise: Why must you never touch a switch with wet hands? | Water can carry electricity into your body | Water makes the switch go rusty straight away | Wet hands make the light too bright | The switch will turn itself off
exercise: Where is it safe to fly a kite? | In an open field far from power lines | Next to a busy railway line with lots of trains | Beside an electricity substation | Under overhead power lines
exercise: You see a power line lying on the ground after a storm. What should you do? | Stay well away and tell an adult at once | Move it aside with a long stick | Step over it carefully and walk on | Touch it quickly to find out if it is still live
exercise: Your friend is stuck to a faulty machine and getting a shock. What should be done first? | Switch off the power at the main switch | Pull your friend away by the arm | Throw a bucket of water over them to cool them down | Wait for the shock to stop by itself
exercise: Why should you not climb an electricity pylon? | The very high voltage cables at the top can kill you | The pylon is too slippery to climb | The pylon might fall over in the wind | Pylons are private property only
exercise: Why should you pull out a plug by holding the plug itself, not the cable? | Pulling the cable can damage the wires inside | Holding the plug makes the electricity stop flowing | The cable is too hot to hold safely | Holding the plug makes it work faster
exercise: What is a simple way for a family to save electricity? | Switch off lights and devices that are not in use | Leave the fridge door open to cool the kitchen | Keep the heating on with the windows open | Charge phones all day and night
test: A house is far from a power station. Which is the right order of the electricity's journey? | Power station, pylons, substation, home | Home, substation, pylons, power station | Pylons, home, substation, power station | Substation, power station, home, pylons
test: The lights go out after someone plugs in a broken toaster. What most likely happened? | A circuit breaker cut off the power | The power station has closed for the night | The meter has run out of numbers to count | The bulbs all broke at exactly the same time
test: Which is the safest thing to do in a bathroom? | Keep electrical things away from the water | Use a hair dryer while sitting in a warm bath | Plug a radio in beside the bath | Touch the light switch with wet hands
test: A boy's football lands inside a fenced electricity substation. What should he do? | Leave it and ask an adult to call the electricity company | Climb the fence quickly to get it back | Use a metal pole to drag it out | Wait until dark and then fetch it
test: A family's electricity bill is very high in winter. What is most likely using the most? | Electric heaters | LED lights in every room | A small radio in the kitchen | A doorbell by the front door
test: Why is a frayed cable dangerous? | Bare wires can give a shock or start a fire | It uses up far too much electricity every hour | It makes the device too slow to warm up properly | It makes the plug keep falling out of the socket
test: Which is true about helping someone who is getting an electric shock? | You must cut the power before touching them | You should grab them quickly with both hands | You should pour water on them to cool them | You should leave them alone until it stops
test: Grandma has one socket and wants to plug in a heater, a kettle and a toaster at once with adapters. What is the danger? | The wires could overheat and start a fire | The kettle will boil more slowly | The toaster will make the heater turn cold instead | Nothing, sockets can take any amount
test: Which of these is a safe place to play? | A park far from overhead power lines | A field under high voltage power lines | The yard of an electricity substation | Beside a fallen power cable
test: kite's stuck near the power lines, can i get it down | No, never fly or fetch a kite near power lines; tell an adult | Yes, if you use a long stick to reach it | Yes, if the kite string is dry | Yes, if you wear rubber shoes
test: why'd all the power go off when we plugged in the heater and kettle | A fuse or circuit breaker cut off the power because too much flowed | The heater sucked all the power out of the house | The kettle and heater cancel each other out | The power station turned off our house on purpose
test: Mia says it's fine to touch a switch with wet hands if u're quick. true? | No, water and electricity are dangerous together | Yes, being really quick keeps you safe from any shocks | Yes, but only with light switches | No, but it is fine with damp hands
test: a video said u should grab someone getting a shock to pull them off. right? | No, switch off the power first before touching them | Yes, pull them off as quickly as you can before it gets worse | Yes, but use both hands to pull hard | No, throw water on them first
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a fuse or circuit breaker? | It cuts off the electricity if too much flows | It increases the electricity supplied to a home | It measures the electrical energy a home uses | It lowers the voltage before electricity enters a home
exam: Identify the function of a substation. | It lowers the voltage before electricity goes to homes | It raises the voltage for use in homes | It manufactures electricity from wind | It stores electricity for use at night
exam: It is given that a person is receiving an electric shock. Ascertain the action which must be taken first. | Switch off the power before touching the person | Seize the person firmly by the hand forthwith and pull | Pour water upon the person at once | Pull the person away with wet hands
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the plugging of many devices into one socket. | It can overheat the wires and cause a fire | It reduces the electricity used by each device | It is the safest manner of using a socket | It has no effect upon the wires whatsoever
